		<meta property="file-as" refines="#fulltitle">The House of Rimmon</meta>
		<meta property="title-type" refines="#fulltitle">expanded</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-1">Naaman, the Syrian--Drama</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-1">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-1">Unknown</meta>
		<dc:subject id="subject-2">Religious drama, American</dc:subject>
		<meta property="authority" refines="#subject-2">LCSH</meta>
		<meta property="term" refines="#subject-2">sh85112625</meta>
		<meta property="schema:genre">Drama</meta>
		<meta property="schema:abstract">A Syrian general and a captive maid seek aid from the prophet Elisha.</meta>
		<dc:description>
			&lt;p&gt;After being betrayed by his beloved wife, the commander of the Syrian army, Naaman, finds help from a captive maid, Ruahmah. Cursed with leprosy by his god, Naaman is led by Ruahmah to Elisha, a prophet of her people’s god. Along the way, Naaman is confronted by his own commitments to faith, honor, and his nation.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;In this dramatic adaptation of the story of Naaman and Elisha, &lt;a href="https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/henry-van-dyke-jr"&gt;Henry van Dyke&lt;/a&gt; illuminates a brief but intriguing Biblical narrative, interweaving themes of love, patriotism, and fidelity.&lt;/p&gt;
		</dc:description>
